## sn4.5
sn4.5:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana. | T: Thus have I heard. On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Baraṇasi in the Deer Park at Isipatana.
sn4.5:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn4.5:1.3 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
sn4.5:1.4 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
sn4.5:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn4.5:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, I am freed from all snares, both human and heavenly. | T: “Bhikkhus, I am freed from all snares, both celestial and human.
sn4.5:2.2 | S: You are also freed from all snares, both human and heavenly. | T: You too, bhikkhus, are freed from all snares, both celestial and human.
sn4.5:2.3 | S: Wander forth, mendicants, for the welfare and happiness of the people, out of sympathy for the world, for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of gods and humans. | T: Wander forth, O bhikkhus, for the welfare of the multitude, for the happiness of the multitude, out of compassion for the world, for the good, welfare, and happiness of devas and humans.
sn4.5:2.4 | S: Let not two go by one road. | T: Let not two go the same way.
sn4.5:2.5 | S: Teach the Dhamma that’s good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased. And reveal a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. | T: Teach, O bhikkhus, the Dhamma that is good in the beginning, good in the middle, good in the end, with the right meaning and phrasing. Reveal the perfectly complete and purified holy life.
sn4.5:2.6 | S: There are beings with little dust in their eyes. They’re in decline because they haven’t heard the teaching. | T: There are beings with little dust in their eyes who are falling away because they do not hear the Dhamma.
sn4.5:2.7 | S: There will be those who understand the teaching! | T: There will be those who will understand the Dhamma.
sn4.5:2.8 | S: I will travel to the town of Senā in Uruvelā in order to teach the Dhamma.” | T: I too, bhikkhus, will go to Senanigama in Uruvela in order to teach the Dhamma.”
sn4.5:2.9 | S: Then Māra the Wicked went up to the Buddha and addressed him in verse: | T: Then Mara the Evil One approached the Blessed One and addressed him in verse:
sn4.5:3.1 | S: “You’re bound by all snares, | T: “You are bound by all the snares
sn4.5:3.2 | S: both human and heavenly. | T: Both celestial and human;
sn4.5:3.3 | S: You’re bound by the great bond: | T: You are bound by the great bondage:
sn4.5:3.4 | S: you won’t escape me, ascetic!” | T: You won’t escape me, ascetic!”
sn4.5:4.1 | S: “I’m freed from all snares, | T: The Blessed One: “I am freed from all the snares
sn4.5:4.2 | S: both human and heavenly. | T: Both celestial and human;
sn4.5:4.3 | S: I’m freed from the great bonds; | T: I am freed from the great bondage:
sn4.5:4.4 | S: You’re beaten, terminator!” | T: You’re defeated, End-maker!”
sn4.5:5.1 | S: Then Māra … vanished right there. | T: (none)

## sn5.2
sn5.2:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn5.2:1.2 | S: Then the nun Somā robed up in the morning and, taking her bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then, in the morning, the bhikkhuni Soma dressed and, taking bowl and robe, entered Savatthi for alms.
sn5.2:1.3 | S: She wandered for alms in Sāvatthī. After the meal, on her return from almsround, she went to the Dark Forest for the day’s meditation, | T: When she had walked for alms in Savatthi and had returned from her alms round, after her meal she went to the Blind Men’s Grove for the day’s abiding.
sn5.2:1.4 | S: plunged deep into it, and sat at the root of a tree to meditate. | T: Having plunged into the Blind Men’s Grove, she sat down at the foot of a tree for the day’s abiding.
sn5.2:1.5 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, wanting to make the nun Somā feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make her fall away from immersion, went up to her and addressed her in verse: | T: Then Mara the Evil One, desiring to arouse fear, trepidation, and terror in the bhikkhuni Soma, desiring to make her fall away from concentration, approached her and addressed her in verse:
sn5.2:2.1 | S: “That state’s very challenging; | T: “That state so hard to achieve
sn5.2:2.2 | S: it’s for the sages to attain. | T: Which is to be attained by the seers,
sn5.2:2.3 | S: With her two-inch wisdom, | T: Can’t be attained by a woman With her two-fingered wisdom.”
sn5.2:2.4 | S: it’s not possible for a woman.” | T: (none)
sn5.2:3.1 | S: Then the nun Somā thought, | T: Then it occurred to the bhikkhuni Soma:
sn5.2:3.2 | S: “Who’s speaking this verse, a human or a non-human?” | T: “Now who is this that recited the verse—a human being or a nonhuman being?”
sn5.2:3.3 | S: Then she thought, | T: Then it occurred to her:
sn5.2:3.4 | S: “This is Māra the Wicked, wanting to make me feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make me fall away from immersion!” | T: “This is Mara the Evil One, who has recited the verse desiring to arouse fear, trepidation, and terror in me, desiring to make me fall away from concentration.”
sn5.2:3.5 | S: Then Somā, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, replied to him in verse: | T: Then the bhikkhuni Soma, having understood, “This is Mara the Evil One,” replied to him in verses:
sn5.2:4.1 | S: “What difference does womanhood make | T: “What does womanhood matter at all
sn5.2:4.2 | S: when the mind is serene, | T: When the mind is concentrated well,
sn5.2:4.3 | S: and knowledge is present | T: When knowledge flows on steadily
sn5.2:4.4 | S: as you rightly discern the Dhamma. | T: As one sees correctly into Dhamma.
sn5.2:5.1 | S: Surely someone who might think: | T: “One to whom it might occur,
sn5.2:5.2 | S: ‘I am woman’, or ‘I am man’, | T: ‘I’m a woman’ or ‘I’m a man’
sn5.2:5.3 | S: or ‘I am’ anything at all, | T: Or ‘I’m anything at all’—
sn5.2:5.4 | S: is fit for Māra to address.” | T: Is fit for Mara to address.”
sn5.2:6.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Somā knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing, “The bhikkhuni Soma knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.

## sn5.3
sn5.3:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn5.3:1.2 | S: Then the nun Kisāgotamī robed up in the morning and, taking her bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then, in the morning, the bhikkhuni Kisagotami dressed and, taking bowl and robe, entered Savatthi for alms.
sn5.3:1.3 | S: She wandered for alms in Sāvatthī. After the meal, on her return from almsround, she went to the Dark Forest for the day’s meditation, | T: When she had walked for alms in Savatthi and had returned from her alms round, after her meal she went to the Blind Men’s Grove for the day’s abiding.
sn5.3:1.4 | S: plunged deep into it, and sat at the root of a tree to meditate. | T: Having plunged into the Blind Men’s Grove, she sat down at the foot of a tree for the day’s abiding.
sn5.3:1.5 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, wanting to make the nun Kisāgotamī feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make her fall away from immersion, went up to her and addressed her in verse: | T: Then Mara the Evil One, desiring to arouse fear, trepidation, and terror in the bhikkhuni Kisagotami, desiring to make her fall away from concentration, approached her and addressed her in verse:
sn5.3:2.1 | S: “Why do you sit alone and cry | T: “Why now, when your son is dead, Do you sit alone with tearful face?
sn5.3:2.2 | S: as if your children have died? | T: (none)
sn5.3:2.3 | S: You’ve come to the woods all alone— | T: Having entered the woods all alone,
sn5.3:2.4 | S: are you looking for a man?” | T: Are you on the lookout for a man?”
sn5.3:3.1 | S: Then the nun Kisāgotamī thought, | T: Then it occurred to the bhikkhuni Kisagotami:
sn5.3:3.2 | S: “Who’s speaking this verse, a human or a non-human?” | T: “Now who is this that recited the verse—a human being or a nonhuman being?”
sn5.3:3.3 | S: Then she thought, | T: Then it occurred to her:
sn5.3:3.4 | S: “This is Māra the Wicked, wanting to make me feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make me fall away from immersion!” | T: “This is Mara the Evil One, who has recited the verse desiring to arouse fear, trepidation, and terror in me, desiring to make me fall away from concentration.”
sn5.3:4.1 | S: Then Kisāgotamī, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, replied to him in verse: | T: Then the bhikkhuni Kisagotami, having understood, “This is Mara the Evil One,” replied to him in verses:
sn5.3:5.1 | S: “I’ve got over the death of children, | T: “I’ve gotten past the death of sons;
sn5.3:5.2 | S: and I’m finished with men. | T: With this, the search for men has ended.
sn5.3:5.3 | S: I don’t grieve or lament, | T: I do not sorrow, I do not weep,
sn5.3:5.4 | S: and I’m not afraid of you, sir! | T: Nor do I fear you, friend .
sn5.3:6.1 | S: Relishing is banished in every respect, | T: “Delight everywhere has been destroyed,
sn5.3:6.2 | S: and the mass of darkness is shattered. | T: The mass of darkness has been sundered.
sn5.3:6.3 | S: I’ve defeated the army of death, | T: Having conquered the army of Death,
sn5.3:6.4 | S: and live without defilements.” | T: I dwell without defiling taints.”
sn5.3:7.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Kisāgotamī knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing, “The bhikkhuni Kisagotami knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.

## an4.35
an4.35:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrel sanctuary.
an4.35:1.2 | S: Then Vassakāra the brahmin, a chief minister of Magadha, went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the brahmin Vassakāra, the chief minister of Magadha, approached the Blessed One and exchanged greetings with him.
an4.35:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to the Blessed One:
an4.35:2.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, when someone has four qualities we describe him as a great man with great wisdom. | T: “Master Gotama, we describe someone who possesses four qualities as a great man with great wisdom.
an4.35:2.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.35:2.3 | S: They are very learned in diverse fields of learning. | T: (1) Here, someone is highly learned in the various fields of learning.
an4.35:2.4 | S: They understand the meaning of diverse statements, saying: ‘This is what that statement means; that is what this statement means.’ | T: (2) He understands the meaning of various statements, so that he can say: ‘This is the meaning of this statement; this is the meaning of that one.’
an4.35:2.5 | S: They are mindful, able to remember and recollect what was said and done long ago. | T: (3) He has a good memory; he remembers and recollects what was done and said long ago.
an4.35:2.6 | S: They are deft and tireless in household duties, understanding how to go about things in order to complete and organize the work. | T: (4) He is skillful and diligent in attending to the diverse chores of a householder; he possesses sound judgment about them in order to carry out and arrange them properly.
an4.35:2.7 | S: When someone possesses these four qualities we describe him as a great man with great wisdom. | T: We describe someone who possesses these four qualities as a great man with great wisdom.
an4.35:2.8 | S: If the worthy Gotama agrees with me, please say so. | T: If Master Gotama thinks what I say should be approved, let him approve it.
an4.35:2.9 | S: If he disagrees, please say so.” | T: If he thinks what I say should be rejected, let him reject it.”
an4.35:3.1 | S: “Brahmin, I neither agree nor disagree with you, | T: “I neither approve of your statement, brahmin, nor do I reject it.
an4.35:3.2 | S: but when someone has four qualities I describe him as a great man with great wisdom. | T: Rather, I describe one who possesses four other qualities as a great man with great wisdom.
an4.35:3.3 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.35:3.4 | S: It’s when someone practices for the welfare and happiness of the people. | T: (1) Here, he is practicing for the welfare and happiness of many people;
an4.35:3.5 | S: They’ve established many people in the noble system, that is, the principles of goodness and skillfulness. | T: he is one who has established many people in the noble method, that is, in the goodness of the Dhamma, in the wholesomeness of the Dhamma.
an4.35:3.6 | S: They think what they want to think, and don’t think what they don’t want to think. | T: (2) He thinks whatever he wants to think and does not think what he does not want to think;
an4.35:3.7 | S: They consider what they want to consider, and don’t consider what they don’t want to consider. | T: he intends whatever he wants to intend and does not intend what he does not want to intend;
an4.35:3.8 | S: Thus they have achieved mental mastery of the paths of thought. | T: thus he has attained to mental mastery over the ways of thought.
an4.35:3.9 | S: They get the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when they want, without trouble or difficulty. | T: (3) He gains at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhānas that constitute the higher mind and are pleasant dwellings in this very life.
an4.35:3.10 | S: They realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: (4) With the destruction of the taints, he has realized for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, he dwells in it.
an4.35:3.11 | S: Brahmin, I neither agree nor disagree with you, | T: “I neither approve of your statement, brahmin, nor do I reject it.
an4.35:3.12 | S: but when someone possesses these four qualities I describe him as a great man with great wisdom.” | T: But I describe someone who possesses these four qualities as a great man with great wisdom.”
an4.35:4.1 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Gotama, it’s amazing! | T: “It is astounding and amazing, Master Gotama,
an4.35:4.2 | S: How well said this was by the worthy Gotama! | T: how well this has been stated by Master Gotama.
an4.35:4.3 | S: And we will remember the worthy Gotama as someone who possesses these four qualities. | T: And we consider Master Gotama as one who possesses these four qualities.
an4.35:4.4 | S: For the worthy Gotama practices for the welfare and happiness of the people … | T: (1) For he is practicing for the welfare and happiness of many people;
an4.35:4.5 | P: bahu te janatā ariye ñāye patiṭṭhāpitā, yadidaṁ kalyāṇadhammatā kusaladhammatā. | T: he is one who has established many people in the noble method, that is, in the goodness of the Dhamma, in the wholesomeness of the Dhamma.
an4.35:4.6 | P: Bhavañhi gotamo yaṁ vitakkaṁ ākaṅkhati vitakketuṁ taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakketi, yaṁ vitakkaṁ nākaṅkhati vitakketuṁ na taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakketi, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ ākaṅkhati saṅkappetuṁ taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappeti, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ nākaṅkhati saṅkappetuṁ na taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappeti. | T: (2) He thinks whatever he wants to think and does not think what he does not want to think; he intends whatever he wants to intend and does not intend what he does not want to intend;
an4.35:4.7 | S: The worthy Gotama has achieved mental mastery of the paths of thought. | T: thus he has attained to mental mastery over the ways of thought.
an4.35:4.8 | S: The worthy Gotama gets the four absorptions … when he wants, without trouble or difficulty. | T: (3) He gains at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhānas that constitute the higher mind and are pleasant dwellings in this very life.
an4.35:4.9 | S: The worthy Gotama has realized the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. He lives having realized it with his own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements.” | T: (4) With the destruction of the taints, he has realized for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, he dwells in it.”
an4.35:5.1 | S: “Your words are clearly invasive and intrusive, brahmin. | T: “Surely, brahmin, your words are prying and intrusive.
an4.35:5.2 | S: Nevertheless, I will answer you. | T: Nevertheless, I will answer you.
an4.35:5.3 | S: For I do practice for the welfare and happiness of the people … | T: (1) Indeed, I am practicing for the welfare and happiness of many people;
an4.35:5.4 | P: bahu me janatā ariye ñāye patiṭṭhāpitā, yadidaṁ kalyāṇadhammatā kusaladhammatā. | T: I have established many people in the noble method, that is, in the goodness of the Dhamma, in the wholesomeness of the Dhamma.
an4.35:5.5 | P: Ahañhi, brāhmaṇa, yaṁ vitakkaṁ ākaṅkhāmi vitakketuṁ taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakkemi, yaṁ vitakkaṁ nākaṅkhāmi vitakketuṁ na taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakkemi, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ ākaṅkhāmi saṅkappetuṁ taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappemi, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ nākaṅkhāmi saṅkappetuṁ na taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappemi. | T: (2) I think what I want to think and do not think what I do not want to think; I intend what I want to intend and do not intend what I do not want to intend;
an4.35:5.6 | S: I have achieved mental mastery of the paths of thought. | T: thus I have attained to mental mastery over the ways of thought.
an4.35:5.7 | S: I do get the four absorptions … when I want, without trouble or difficulty. | T: (3) I gain at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhānas that constitute the higher mind and are pleasant dwellings in this very life.
an4.35:5.8 | S: I have realized the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. I live having realized it with my own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: (4) With the destruction of the taints, I have realized for myself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, I dwell in it.”
an4.35:6.1 | S: He discovered release from the snare of death | T: He who found for the sake of all beings release from the snare of death;
an4.35:6.2 | S: for all beings, | T: (none)
an4.35:6.3 | S: and explained the system of the teaching | T: who revealed the Dhamma, the method,
an4.35:6.4 | S: for the welfare of gods and humans. | T: for the benefit of devas and humans;
an4.35:6.5 | S: When they see him or hear him, | T: (none)
an4.35:6.6 | S: many people become confident. | T: he in whom many people gain confidence when they see and listen to him;
an4.35:7.1 | S: He is skilled in what is the path <j>and what is not the path, | T: the one skilled in the path and what is not the path,
an4.35:7.2 | S: he has completed the task <j>and is free of defilements. | T: the taintless one who accomplished his task;
an4.35:7.3 | S: The Buddha, bearing his final body, | T: the Enlightened One bearing his final body
an4.35:7.4 | S: is called ‘a great man, of great wisdom’.” | T: is called “a great man of great wisdom.”
an4.35:7.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.39
an3.39:1.1 | S: “My lifestyle was delicate, mendicants, most delicate, extremely delicate. | T: “Bhikkhus, I was delicately nurtured, most delicately nurtured, extremely delicately nurtured.
an3.39:1.2 | S: In my father’s home, lotus ponds were made just for me. | T: At my father’s residence lotus ponds were made just for my enjoyment:
an3.39:1.3 | S: In some, blue water lilies blossomed, while in others, there were pink or white lotuses, just for my benefit. | T: in one of them blue lotuses bloomed, in another red lotuses, and in a third white lotuses.
an3.39:1.4 | S: I only used sandalwood from Kāsi, and my turbans, jackets, sarongs, and upper robes also came from Kāsi. | T: I used no sandalwood unless it came from Kāsi and my headdress, jacket, lower garment, and upper garment were made of cloth from Kāsi.
an3.39:1.5 | S: And a white parasol was held over me night and day, with the thought: | T: By day and by night a white canopy was held over me
an3.39:1.6 | S: ‘Don’t let cold, heat, grass, dust, or damp bother him.’ | T: so that cold and heat, dust, grass, and dew would not settle on me.
an3.39:2.1 | S: I had three stilt longhouses—one for the winter, one for the summer, and one for the rainy season. | T: “I had three mansions: one for the winter, one for the summer, and one for the rainy season.
an3.39:2.2 | S: I stayed in a stilt longhouse without coming downstairs for the four months of the rainy season, where I was entertained by musicians—none of them men. | T: I spent the four months of the rains in the rainy-season mansion, being entertained by musicians, none of whom were male, and I did not leave the mansion.
an3.39:2.3 | S: While the bondservants, workers, and staff in other houses are given sour gruel with false black pepper to eat, in my father’s home they are given fine rice with meat. | T: While in other people’s homes slaves, workers, and servants are given broken rice together with sour gruel for their meals, in my father’s residence they were given choice hill rice, meat, and boiled rice.
an3.39:3.1 | S: Amid such prosperity and such a delicate lifestyle, I thought: | T: (1) “Amid such splendor and a delicate life, it occurred to me:
an3.39:3.2 | S: ‘When an unlearned ordinary person—who is liable to grow old, not being exempt from old age—sees someone else who is old, they’re horrified, repelled, and disgusted, overlooking the fact that they themselves are in the same situation. | T: ‘An uninstructed worldling, though himself subject to old age, not exempt from old age, feels repelled, humiliated, and disgusted when he sees another who is old, overlooking his own situation.
an3.39:3.3 | S: But since I, too, am liable to grow old, it would not be appropriate for me to be horrified, embarrassed, and disgusted, when I see someone else who is old.’ | T: Now I too am subject to old age and am not exempt from old age. Such being the case, if I were to feel repelled, humiliated, and disgusted when seeing another who is old, that would not be proper for me.’
an3.39:3.4 | S: Reflecting like this, I entirely gave up the vanity of youth. | T: When I reflected thus, my intoxication with youth was completely abandoned.
an3.39:4.1 | S: ‘When an unlearned ordinary person—who is liable to get sick, not being exempt from sickness—sees someone else who is sick, they’re horrified, repelled, and disgusted, overlooking the fact that they themselves are in the same situation. | T: (2) “Again, it occurred to me: ‘An uninstructed worldling, though himself subject to illness, not exempt from illness, feels repelled, humiliated, and disgusted when he sees another who is ill, overlooking his own situation.
an3.39:4.2 | S: But since I, too, am liable to get sick, it would not be appropriate for me to be horrified, embarrassed, and disgusted, when I see someone else who is sick.’ | T: Now I too am subject to illness and am not exempt from illness. Such being the case, if I were to feel repelled, humiliated, and disgusted when seeing another who is ill, that would not be proper for me.’
an3.39:4.3 | S: Reflecting like this, I entirely gave up the vanity of health. | T: When I reflected thus, my intoxication with health was completely abandoned.
an3.39:5.1 | S: ‘When an unlearned ordinary person—who is liable to die, not being exempt from death—sees someone else who is dead, they’re horrified, repelled, and disgusted, overlooking the fact that they themselves are in the same situation. | T: (3) “Again, it occurred to me: ‘An uninstructed worldling, though himself subject to death, not exempt from death, feels repelled, humiliated, and disgusted when he sees another who has died, overlooking his own situation.
an3.39:5.2 | S: But since I, too, am liable to die, it would not be appropriate for me to be horrified, embarrassed, and disgusted, when I see someone else who is dead.’ | T: Now I too am subject to death and am not exempt from death. Such being the case, if I were to feel repelled, humiliated, and disgusted when seeing another who has died, that would not be proper for me.’
an3.39:5.3 | S: Reflecting like this, I entirely gave up the vanity of life. | T: When I reflected thus, my intoxication with life was completely abandoned.
an3.39:6.1 | S: There are these three vanities. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, these three kinds of intoxication.
an3.39:6.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.39:6.3 | S: The vanity of youth, of health, and of life. | T: Intoxication with youth, intoxication with health, and intoxication with life.
an3.39:6.4 | S: Intoxicated with the vanity of youth, an unlearned ordinary person does bad things by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: (1) An uninstructed worldling, intoxicated with youth, engages in misconduct by body, speech, and mind.
an3.39:6.5 | S: When their body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: With the breakup of the body, after death, he is reborn in the plane of misery, in a bad destination, in the lower world, in hell.
an3.39:6.6 | S: Intoxicated with the vanity of health … | T: (2) An uninstructed worldling, intoxicated with health, engages in misconduct by body, speech, and mind. With the breakup of the body, after death, he is reborn in the plane of misery, in a bad destination, in the lower world, in hell.
an3.39:6.7 | S: Intoxicated with the vanity of life, an unlearned ordinary person does bad things by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: (3) An uninstructed worldling, intoxicated with life, engages in misconduct by body, speech, and mind.
an3.39:6.8 | S: When their body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: With the breakup of the body, after death, he is reborn in the plane of misery, in a bad destination, in the lower world, in hell.
an3.39:7.1 | S: Intoxicated with the vanity of youth, health, or life, a mendicant resigns the training and returns to a lesser life. | T: “Intoxicated with youth, a bhikkhu gives up the training and reverts to the lower life;
an3.39:7.2 | P: Ārogyamadamatto vā, bhikkhave, bhikkhu …pe… | T: or intoxicated with health, he gives up the training and reverts to the lower life;
an3.39:7.3 | P: jīvitamadamatto vā, bhikkhave, bhikkhu sikkhaṁ paccakkhāya hīnāyāvattatīti. | T: or intoxicated with life, he gives up the training and reverts to the lower life.
an3.39:8.1 | S: For others, sickness is natural, | T: “Worldlings subject to illness,
an3.39:8.2 | S: and so are old age and death. | T: old age, and death,
an3.39:8.3 | S: Though this is how their nature is, | T: (none)
an3.39:8.4 | S: ordinary people feel disgusted. | T: are disgusted by other people who exist in accordance with their nature.
an3.39:9.1 | S: If I were to be disgusted | T: “If I were to become disgusted
an3.39:9.2 | S: with creatures whose nature is such, | T: with beings who have such a nature,
an3.39:9.3 | S: it would not be appropriate for me, | T: that would not be proper for me
an3.39:9.4 | S: since my life is just the same. | T: since I too have the same nature.
an3.39:10.1 | S: Living in such a way, | T: “While I was dwelling thus,
an3.39:10.2 | S: I understood the truth without attachments. | T: having known the state without acquisitions,
an3.39:10.3 | S: I mastered all vanities— | T: I overcame all intoxications—
an3.39:10.4 | S: of health, of youth, | T: intoxication with health, with youth,
an3.39:11.1 | S: and even of life— | T: and with life—
an3.39:11.2 | S: seeing renunciation as sanctuary. | T: having seen security in renunciation.
an3.39:11.3 | S: Zeal sprang up in me | T: “Zeal then arose in me
an3.39:11.4 | S: as I looked to extinguishment. | T: as I clearly saw nibbāna.
an3.39:12.1 | S: Now I’m unable | T: Now I am incapable
an3.39:12.2 | S: to indulge in sensual pleasures; | T: of indulging in sensual pleasures.
an3.39:12.3 | S: there’s no turning back, | T: Relying on the spiritual life, never will I turn back.”
an3.39:12.4 | S: I’m committed to the spiritual life.” | T: (none)

