## sn16.13
sn16.13:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn16.13:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park.
sn16.13:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Mahākassapa went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Mahakassapa approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn16.13:1.4 | S: “What is the cause, sir, what is the reason why there used to be fewer training rules but more enlightened mendicants? | T: “Venerable sir, what is the reason, what is the cause, why formerly there were fewer training rules but more bhikkhus were established in final knowledge,
sn16.13:1.5 | S: And what is the cause, what is the reason why these days there are more training rules and fewer enlightened mendicants?” | T: while now there are more training rules but fewer bhikkhus are established in final knowledge?”
sn16.13:1.6 | S: “That’s how it is, Kassapa. When sentient beings are in decline and the true teaching is disappearing there are more training rules and fewer enlightened mendicants. | T: “That’s the way it is, Kassapa. When beings are deteriorating and the true Dhamma is disappearing there are more training rules but fewer bhikkhus are established in final knowledge.
sn16.13:1.7 | S: The true teaching doesn’t disappear as long as the counterfeit of the true teaching hasn’t appeared in the world. | T: Kassapa, the true Dhamma does not disappear so long as a counterfeit of the true Dhamma has not arisen in the world.
sn16.13:1.8 | S: But when the counterfeit of the true teaching appears in the world then the true teaching disappears. | T: But when a counterfeit of the true Dhamma arises in the world, then the true Dhamma disappears.
sn16.13:2.1 | S: It’s like native gold, which doesn’t disappear as long as counterfeit gold hasn’t appeared in the world. | T: “Just as, Kassapa, gold does not disappear so long as counterfeit gold has not arisen in the world,
sn16.13:2.2 | S: But when counterfeit gold appears in the world then native gold disappears. | T: but when counterfeit gold arises then true gold disappears,
sn16.13:2.3 | S: In the same way, the true teaching doesn’t disappear as long as the counterfeit of the true teaching hasn’t appeared in the world. | T: so the true Dhamma does not disappear so long as a counterfeit of the true Dhamma has not arisen in the world,
sn16.13:2.4 | S: But when the counterfeit of the true teaching appears in the world then the true teaching disappears. | T: but when a counterfeit of the true Dhamma arises in the world, then the true Dhamma disappears.
sn16.13:3.1 | S: It’s not the elements of earth, water, fire, or air that make the true teaching disappear. | T: “It is not the earth element, Kassapa, that causes the true Dhamma to disappear, nor the water element, nor the heat element, nor the air element.
sn16.13:3.2 | S: Rather, it’s the futile people who appear right here that make the true teaching disappear. | T: It is the senseless people who arise right here who cause the true Dhamma to disappear.
sn16.13:3.3 | S: The true teaching doesn’t disappear like a ship that sinks all at once. | T: “The true Dhamma does not disappear all at once in the way a ship sinks.
sn16.13:3.4 | P: na kho, kassapa, evaṁ saddhammassa antaradhānaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn16.13:4.1 | S: There are five detrimental things that lead to the decline and disappearance of the true teaching. | T: There are, Kassapa, five detrimental things that lead to the decay and disappearance of the true Dhamma.
sn16.13:4.2 | S: What five? | T: What are the five?
sn16.13:4.3 | S: It’s when the monks, nuns, laymen, and laywomen lack respect and reverence for the Teacher, the teaching, the Saṅgha, the training, and immersion. | T: Here the bhikkhus, the bhikkhunīs, the male lay followers, and the female lay followers dwell without reverence and deference towards the Teacher; they dwell without reverence and deference towards the Dhamma; they dwell without reverence and deference towards the Saṅgha; they dwell without reverence and deference towards the training; they dwell without reverence and deference towards concentration.
sn16.13:4.4 | S: These five detrimental things lead to the decline and disappearance of the true teaching. | T: These, Kassapa, are the five detrimental things that lead to the decay and disappearance of the true Dhamma.
sn16.13:5.1 | S: There are five things that lead to the continuation, persistence, and enduring of the true teaching. | T: “There are five things, Kassapa, that lead to the longevity of the true Dhamma, to its nondecay and nondisappearance.
sn16.13:5.2 | S: What five? | T: What are the five?
sn16.13:5.3 | S: It’s when the monks, nuns, laymen, and laywomen maintain respect and reverence for the Teacher, the teaching, the Saṅgha, the training, and immersion. | T: Here the bhikkhus, the bhikkhunīs, the male lay followers, and the female lay followers dwell with reverence and deference towards the Teacher; they dwell with reverence and deference towards the Dhamma; they dwell with reverence and deference towards the Saṅgha; they dwell with reverence and deference towards the training; they dwell with reverence and deference towards concentration.
sn16.13:5.4 | S: These five things lead to the continuation, persistence, and enduring of the true teaching.” | T: These, Kassapa, are the five things that lead to the longevity of the true Dhamma, to its nondecay and nondisappearance.”
sn16.13:5.5 | P: Terasamaṁ. | T: (none)
sn16.13:5.6 | P: Kassapavaggo paṭhamo. | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.1 | P: Santuṭṭhañca anottappī, | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.2 | P: candūpamaṁ kulūpakaṁ; | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.3 | P: Jiṇṇaṁ tayo ca ovādā, | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.4 | P: jhānābhiññā upassayaṁ; | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.5 | P: Cīvaraṁ paraṁmaraṇaṁ, | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.6 | P: saddhammappatirūpakanti. | T: (none)
sn16.13:6.7 | S: The Linked Discourses with Kassapa are complete. | T: (none)

## sn22.37
sn22.37:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.37:1.2 | S: And then Venerable Ānanda … | T: Then the Venerable Ānanda approached the Blessed One….
sn22.37:1.3 | S: sitting to one side, the Buddha said to him: | T: The Blessed One then said to the Venerable Ānanda as he was sitting to one side:
sn22.37:2.1 | S: “Ānanda, suppose they were to ask you: | T: “If, Ānanda, they were to ask you:
sn22.37:2.2 | S: ‘Reverend Ānanda, what are the things for which arising is evident, vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident?’ | T: ‘Friend Ānanda, what are the things of which an arising is discerned, a vanishing is discerned, an alteration of that which stands is discerned?’—
sn22.37:2.3 | S: How would you answer?” | T: being asked thus, how would you answer?”
sn22.37:2.4 | S: “Sir, suppose they were to ask me: | T: “Venerable sir, if they were to ask me this,
sn22.37:2.5 | S: ‘What are the things for which arising is evident, vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident?’ | T: (none)
sn22.37:2.6 | S: I’d answer like this: | T: I would answer thus:
sn22.37:2.7 | S: ‘Reverend, the arising of form is evident, its vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident. | T: ‘Friends, with form an arising is discerned, a vanishing is discerned, an alteration of that which stands is discerned.
sn22.37:2.8 | S: The arising of feeling … | T: With feeling …
sn22.37:2.9 | S: perception … | T: perception …
sn22.37:2.10 | S: choices … | T: volitional formations …
sn22.37:2.11 | S: consciousness is evident, its vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident. | T: consciousness an arising is discerned, a vanishing is discerned, an alteration of that which stands is discerned.
sn22.37:2.12 | S: These are the things for which arising is evident, vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident.’ | T: These, friends, are the things of which an arising is discerned, a vanishing is discerned, an alteration of that which stands is discerned.’
sn22.37:2.13 | S: That’s how I’d answer such a question.” | T: Being asked thus, venerable sir, I would answer in such a way.”
sn22.37:3.1 | S: “Good, good, Ānanda. | T: “Good, good, Ānanda!
sn22.37:3.2 | S: The arising of form is evident, its vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident. | T: With form, Ānanda, an arising is discerned, a vanishing is discerned, an alteration of that which stands is discerned.
sn22.37:3.3 | S: The arising of feeling … | T: With feeling …
sn22.37:3.4 | S: perception … | T: perception …
sn22.37:3.5 | S: choices … | T: volitional formations …
sn22.37:3.6 | S: consciousness is evident, its vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident. | T: consciousness an arising is discerned, a vanishing is discerned, an alteration of that which stands is discerned.
sn22.37:3.7 | S: These are the things for which arising is evident, vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident. | T: These, Ānanda, are the things of which an arising is discerned, a vanishing is discerned, an alteration of that which stands is discerned.
sn22.37:3.8 | S: That’s how you should answer such a question.” | T: Being asked thus, Ānanda, you should answer in such a way.”
sn22.37:3.9 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.24
an4.24:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāketa in Kāḷaka’s monastery. | T: Thus have I heard. On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Sāketa, at Kāḷaka’s Park.
an4.24:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus:
an4.24:1.3 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
an4.24:1.4 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
an4.24:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
an4.24:2.1 | S: “In this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—whatever is seen, heard, thought, known, attained, sought, and explored by the mind: that I know. | T: “Bhikkhus, in this world with its devas, Māra, and Brahmā, among this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans, whatever is seen, heard, sensed, cognized, reached, sought after, examined by the mind—that I know.
an4.24:3.1 | S: In this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—whatever is seen, heard, thought, known, attained, sought, and explored by the mind: that I have insight into. | T: “Bhikkhus, in this world with its devas, Māra, and Brahmā, among this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans, whatever is seen, heard, sensed, cognized, reached, sought after, examined by the mind—that I have directly known.
an4.24:3.2 | S: That has been known by a Realized One, but a Realized One is not subject to it. | T: It has been known by the Tathāgata, but the Tathāgata did not become subservient to it.
an4.24:4.1 | S: If I were to say that ‘I do not know … the world with its gods’, I would be lying. | T: “Bhikkhus, if I were to say, ‘In this world with its devas … whatever is seen, heard, sensed, cognized, reached, sought after, examined by the mind—that I do not know,’ that would be a falsehood on my part.
an4.24:5.1 | S: If I were to say that ‘I both know and do not know … the world with its gods’, that would be just the same. | T: “Bhikkhus, if I were to say, ‘In this world with its devas … whatever is seen, heard, sensed, cognized, reached, sought after, examined by the mind—that I both know and do not know,’ that too would be just the same.
an4.24:6.1 | S: If I were to say that ‘I neither know nor do not know … the world with its gods’, that would be my fault. | T: “Bhikkhus, if I were to say, ‘In this world with its devas … whatever is seen, heard, sensed, cognized, reached, sought after, examined by the mind—that I neither know nor do not know,’ that would be a fault on my part.
an4.24:7.1 | S: So a Realized One sees what is to be seen, but does not conceive what is seen, does not conceive what is unseen, does not conceive what is to be seen, and does not conceive a seer. | T: (1) “So, having seen what can be seen, the Tathāgata does not misconceive the seen, does not misconceive the unseen, does not misconceive what can be seen, does not misconceive one who sees.
an4.24:7.2 | S: He hears what is to be heard, but does not conceive what is heard, does not conceive what is unheard, does not conceive what is to be heard, and does not conceive a hearer. | T: (2) Having heard what can be heard, he does not misconceive the heard, does not misconceive the unheard, does not misconceive what can be heard, does not misconceive one who hears.
an4.24:7.3 | S: He thinks what is to be thought, but does not conceive what is thought, does not conceive what is not thought, does not conceive what is to be thought, and does not conceive a thinker. | T: (3) Having sensed what can be sensed, he does not misconceive the sensed, does not misconceive the unsensed, does not misconceive what can be sensed, does not misconceive one who senses.
an4.24:7.4 | S: He knows what is to be known, but does not conceive what is known, does not conceive what is unknown, does not conceive what is to be known, and does not conceive a knower. | T: (4) Having cognized what can be cognized, he does not misconceive the cognized, does not misconceive the uncognized, does not misconceive what can be cognized, does not misconceive one who cognizes.
an4.24:7.5 | S: Since a Realized One is unaffected in the midst of things that ought be seen, heard, thought, and known, he is the unaffected one. | T: “Thus, bhikkhus, being ever stable among things seen, heard, sensed, and cognized, the Tathāgata is a stable one.
an4.24:7.6 | S: And I say that there is no better or finer poise than this. | T: And, I say, there is no stable one more excellent or sublime than that stable one.”
an4.24:8.1 | S: Such a one does not take anything | T: Amid those who are self-constrained, the Stable One
an4.24:8.2 | S: seen, heard, or thought to be ultimately true or false. | T: would not posit as categorically true or false anything seen, heard, or sensed,
an4.24:8.3 | S: But others get attached, thinking it’s the truth, | T: clung to and considered truth by others.
an4.24:8.4 | S: limited by their preconceptions. | T: (none)
an4.24:9.1 | S: Since they’ve seen this dart | T: Since they have already seen this dart
an4.24:9.2 | S: to which people are attached and cling, | T: to which people cling and adhere,
an4.24:9.3 | S: saying, ‘I know, I see, that’s how it is’, | T: saying “I know, I see, it is just so,”
an4.24:9.4 | S: the Realized Ones have no attachments.” | T: the Tathāgatas cling to nothing.
an4.24:9.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn36.31
sn36.31:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there is rapture of the flesh, rapture not  of the flesh, and rapture even more spiritual than that not of the flesh. | T: “Bhikkhus, there is carnal rapture, there is spiritual rapture, there is rapture more spiritual than the spiritual.
sn36.31:1.2 | S: There is pleasure of the flesh, pleasure not of the flesh, and pleasure even more spiritual than that not of the flesh. | T: There is carnal happiness, there is spiritual happiness, there is happiness more spiritual than the spiritual.
sn36.31:1.3 | S: There is equanimity of the flesh, equanimity not of the flesh, and equanimity even more spiritual than that not of the flesh. | T: There is carnal equanimity, there is spiritual equanimity, there is equanimity more spiritual than the spiritual.
sn36.31:1.4 | S: There is liberation of the flesh, liberation not of the flesh, and liberation even more spiritual than that not of the flesh. | T: There is carnal deliverance, there is spiritual deliverance, there is deliverance more spiritual than the spiritual.
sn36.31:1.5 | S: And what is rapture of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is carnal rapture?
sn36.31:1.6 | S: There are these five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: There are, bhikkhus, these five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn36.31:1.7 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn36.31:1.8 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. Sounds … Smells … Tastes … | T: Forms cognizable by the eye …
sn36.31:1.9 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile objects cognizable by the body that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn36.31:1.10 | S: These are the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: These are the five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn36.31:1.11 | S: The rapture that arises from these five kinds of sensual stimulation is called rapture of the flesh. | T: The rapture that arises in dependence on these five cords of sensual pleasure: this is called carnal rapture.
sn36.31:2.1 | S: And what is rapture not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is spiritual rapture?
sn36.31:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: Here, secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unwholesome states, a bhikkhu enters and dwells in the first jhana, which is accompanied by thought and examination, with rapture and happiness born of seclusion.
sn36.31:2.3 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, they enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: With the subsiding of thought and examination, he enters and dwells in the second jhana, which has internal confidence and unification of mind, is without thought and examination, and has rapture and happiness born of concentration.
sn36.31:2.4 | S: This is called rapture not of the flesh. | T: This is called spiritual rapture.
sn36.31:3.1 | S: And what is rapture even more spiritual than that not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is rapture more spiritual than the spiritual?
sn36.31:3.2 | S: When a mendicant who has ended the defilements reviews their mind free from greed, hate, and delusion, rapture arises. This is called rapture even more spiritual than that not of the flesh. | T: When a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed reviews his mind liberated from lust, liberated from hatred, liberated from delusion, there arises rapture. This is called rapture more spiritual than the spiritual.
sn36.31:4.1 | S: And what is pleasure of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is carnal happiness?
sn36.31:4.2 | S: Mendicants, there are these five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: There are, bhikkhus, these five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn36.31:4.3 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn36.31:4.4 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. Sounds … Smells … Tastes … | T: Forms cognizable by the eye …
sn36.31:4.5 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile objects cognizable by the body that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn36.31:4.6 | S: These are the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: These are the five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn36.31:4.7 | S: The pleasure and happiness that arise from these five kinds of sensual stimulation is called pleasure of the flesh. | T: The happiness that arises in dependence on these five cords of sensual pleasure: this is called carnal happiness.
sn36.31:5.1 | S: And what is pleasure not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is spiritual happiness?
sn36.31:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: Here, bhikkhus, secluded from sensual pleasures … a bhikkhu enters and dwells in the first jhana …
sn36.31:5.3 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, they enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: the second jhana….
sn36.31:5.4 | S: And with the fading away of rapture, they enter and remain in the third absorption, where they meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: With the fading away as well of rapture, he dwells equanimous and, mindful and clearly comprehending, he experiences happiness with the body; he enters and dwells in the third jhana of which the noble ones declare: ‘He is equanimous, mindful, one who dwells happily.’
sn36.31:5.5 | S: This is called pleasure not of the flesh. | T: This is called spiritual happiness.
sn36.31:6.1 | S: And what is pleasure even more spiritual than that not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is happiness more spiritual than the spiritual?
sn36.31:6.2 | S: When a mendicant who has ended the defilements reviews their mind free from greed, hate, and delusion, pleasure and happiness arises. This is called pleasure even more spiritual than that not of the flesh. | T: When a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed reviews his mind liberated from lust, liberated from hatred, liberated from delusion, there arises happiness. This is called happiness more spiritual than the spiritual.
sn36.31:7.1 | S: And what is equanimity of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is carnal equanimity?
sn36.31:7.2 | S: There are these five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: There are, bhikkhus, these five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn36.31:7.3 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn36.31:7.4 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. Sounds … Smells … Tastes … | T: Forms cognizable by the eye …
sn36.31:7.5 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile objects cognizable by the body that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn36.31:7.6 | S: These are the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: These are the five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn36.31:7.7 | S: The equanimity that arises from these five kinds of sensual stimulation is called equanimity of the flesh. | T: The equanimity that arises in dependence on these five cords of sensual pleasure: this is called carnal equanimity.
sn36.31:8.1 | S: And what is equanimity not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is spiritual equanimity?
sn36.31:8.2 | S: It’s when, with the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, a mendicant enters and remains in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: With the abandoning of pleasure and pain, and with the previous passing away of joy and displeasure, a bhikkhu enters and dwells in the fourth jhana, which is neither painful nor pleasant and includes the purification of mindfulness by equanimity.
sn36.31:8.3 | S: This is called equanimity not of the flesh. | T: (none)
sn36.31:9.1 | S: And what is equanimity even more spiritual than that not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is equanimity more spiritual than the spiritual?
sn36.31:9.2 | S: When a mendicant who has ended the defilements reviews their mind free from greed, hate, and delusion, equanimity arises. This is called equanimity even more spiritual than that not of the flesh. | T: When a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed reviews his mind liberated from lust, liberated from hatred, liberated from delusion, there arises equanimity. This is called equanimity more spiritual than the spiritual.
sn36.31:10.1 | S: And what is liberation of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is carnal deliverance?
sn36.31:10.2 | S: Liberation connected with form is liberation of the flesh. | T: Deliverance connected with the form sphere is carnal deliverance.
sn36.31:11.1 | S: And what is liberation not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is spiritual deliverance?
sn36.31:11.2 | S: Liberation connected with the formless is liberation not of the flesh. | T: Deliverance connected with the formless sphere is spiritual deliverance.
sn36.31:12.1 | S: And what is liberation even more spiritual than that not of the flesh? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is deliverance more spiritual than the spiritual?
sn36.31:12.2 | S: When a mendicant who has ended the defilements reviews their mind free from greed, hate, and delusion, liberation arises. This is called liberation even more spiritual than that not of the flesh.” | T: When a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed reviews his mind liberated from lust, liberated from hatred, liberated from delusion, there arises deliverance. This is called deliverance more spiritual than the spiritual.”
sn36.31:12.3 | P: Ekādasamaṁ. | T: (none)
sn36.31:12.4 | P: Aṭṭhasatapariyāyavaggo tatiyo. | T: (none)
sn36.31:13.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn36.31:13.1 | P: Sīvakaaṭṭhasataṁ bhikkhu, | T: (none)
sn36.31:13.2 | P: pubbe ñāṇañca bhikkhunā; | T: (none)
sn36.31:13.3 | P: Samaṇabrāhmaṇā tīṇi, | T: (none)
sn36.31:13.4 | P: suddhikañca nirāmisanti. | T: (none)
sn36.31:13.5 | S: The Linked Discourses on feelings are complete. | T: (none)


